 \contentsline {chapter}{Figure}{\textbf {Page}}{}
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces Comparison of no drift versus drift scenarios. Left: When distributions overlap, the discriminative classifier cannot distinguish data sources (AUC $\approx $ 0.5). Right: When drift occurs, distributions shift and the classifier can distinguish sources (AUC $>>$ 0.5).}}{6}{figure.caption.15}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces High-level overview of the EADD methodology, illustrating the four core conceptual stages.}}{27}{figure.caption.52}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces Detailed architecture of the Enhanced Adversarial Drift Detection (EADD) methodology, matching the pipeline design used in the JCSEE paper: adaptive windowing, discriminative detection, ASPT significance gating, and SHAP+DSI diagnostics.}}{28}{figure.caption.53}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces Detailed flowchart of the EADD methodology with mathematical formalisms. The process flows from data windowing (1), through discriminative classification (2) and performance measurement (3), to statistical validation via ASPT. If drift is significant, the framework proceeds to SHAP-based diagnosis and severity scoring with DSI, culminating in an actionable MLOps report and prescription. Pipeline components map to three thesis contributions: Contributions~1--2 ($H_{\text {pred}}$ and ASPT) form Contribution~1; Contributions~3--4 (SHAP Localisation and DSI) form Contribution~2; Contribution~3 is the comprehensive experimental evaluation.}}{29}{figure.caption.54}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Heatmaps of detection performance on synthetic drift. Top: Mean Time to Detection (lower is better; white cells indicate complete miss). Bottom: False Alarm count (lower is better).}}{52}{figure.caption.96}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Bell curves showing feature distributions before (blue) and after (red) drift injection. Top: Abrupt drift with clear separation. Bottom: Gradual drift with overlapping distributions---the subtle shift that D3, SPLL, and UDetect all failed to detect.}}{53}{figure.caption.97}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces Time-series comparison of all eight detectors. Top: Abrupt drift---most detectors respond near the true drift point (dashed line). Bottom: Gradual drift---only EADD, BNDM, CSDDM, IBDD, and OCDD detect the shift; D3, SPLL, and UDetect remain silent.}}{54}{figure.caption.98}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces Heatmaps of real-world benchmark performance. Top: Mean Time to Detection (lower is better; white cells indicate missed drifts or timeout). Bottom: Missed Detection Rate (0.0 is perfect).}}{58}{figure.caption.106}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.5}{\ignorespaces Time-series comparison on benchmark datasets from the multi-detector supplementary run. Top: WaveformDrift2 (semi-synthetic)---EADD detects all three drift points (MTD=99), outperforming D3 (MTD=179). Bottom: SineClusters (semi-synthetic)---IBDD detects rapidly but with many excess detections, illustrating the sensitivity--specificity trade-off.}}{59}{figure.caption.107}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.6}{\ignorespaces Feature distribution bell curves. Top: WaveformDrift2 (semi-synthetic) showing multi-dimensional distributional shift across 21 features. Bottom: InsectsAbrupt showing abrupt distributional changes across 33 features.}}{60}{figure.caption.108}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.7}{\ignorespaces Combined bell curve and SHAP attribution analysis for three controlled scenarios. Top: Univariate drift---F3 dominates at 52.3\%. Middle: Subset drift---F5, F2, F7 collectively account for 65\%. Bottom: Multivariate drift---importance distributed evenly, max 14.6\%.}}{65}{figure.caption.119}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.8}{\ignorespaces Top: SHAP attribution accuracy across all three scenarios (P@k = R@k = F1@k = 1.0 for all). Bottom: Diagnostic capability comparison---EADD is the only detector providing feature-level attribution and automated prescriptions.}}{66}{figure.caption.120}%
